A||||20||MooCow||Help with tinting windows?||||1||
Z||000000||MooCow||01-25-2001||11:54 AM||||Well I plan on tinting my windows tomorrow and I was wondering if you have any tips.<P>                                 Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||01-25-2001||12:08 PM||||Make sure the tint is legal.  Some states/localities have rules as to the degree of tint you are allowed.  ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Fleabitdog||01-25-2001||01:49 PM||||Also make sure aftermarket tint is legal. About six years ago I lived in Pa. and was pulled for my tint. The degree of tint was legal but the cop said aftermarket tint was illegal and gave me a $95.00 fine.I did not do the tint myself. It was done at a shop in Ft Lauderdale and they did a very good job. Cost about a hundred bucks back then.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MooCow||01-25-2001||02:01 PM||||Any tips on the installation?<P>                          Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000004||stimpy||01-25-2001||02:33 PM||||Yeah. Pay someone else to do it! Seriously. That is not a glib smartass comment. Tinting the Fiero is a pain in the ayass. If you insist on DIY, you will need to remove both seatbelts, the interior trim panels, and try not to get carpet fibers on the tint as you put it in.  And if you do it wrong, you got no one to blame but yourself. <P>A reputable shop shouldn't charge more then $100 to do rear and side windows with a lifetime guarantee. Everytime I've atempted tinting, I've thrown out $40 in materials and spent $60 at chriopractors recovering from playing Twister in the tiny interior.<P>Pay a professional. It's so worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||01-25-2001||02:37 PM||||Stimpy stole my post.  Tinting any car is a pain in the butt and unless you are a professional, it will bubble no matter what and you won't save that much money.  <P>There is a place around me called D&L Window Tinting, they use this tint called "Sunguard" where you can hardly even tell its on from inside the car but from outside its obvious.  They also have a MD state license so its not illegal.  To make it even better they offer a lifetime warranty against peeling, cracking, bubbling or turning purple.  For 120 bucks and 2 hours time I was quoted, its WELL WORTH IT!<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||KissMySSFiero||01-25-2001||04:20 PM||||if you are going DIY route.  wait til its warmer. correct me if im wrong but all the tints i have seen on the shelves state that it should be above 40 deg. for three consecutive days.  your in MI and i know its cold. i did mine here in fla and the gluc looks hazy in some area.  mostly cuz i had no idea what i was doin,  and will pay someone to do it right when its warmer and i feel like takin the stuff i put on wrong,  off of my car.  good luch whichever way you go.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:KissMySSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:KissMySSFiero@netzero.net">KissMySSFiero@netzero.net</A>   <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">       <BR>"There's a fine line between love and beer"<BR><A HREF="http://expage.com/kissmyssfiero" TARGET=_blank>KissMySSFiero.mywebsite.hehe</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||01-25-2001||05:44 PM||||I'm taking everything out even the side windows and doing it indoors.  It can't be that hard to tint.  I donb't have the money to spend $100 some bucks on it.  I got everything I needed for $30.  I will let you know how it goes.  <BR>                             Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||01-25-2001||06:04 PM||||"Experience keeps a dear school, but fools will learn in no other"<P>- Ben Franklin||||reg||1
Z||000009||zeusrekning||01-25-2001||06:32 PM||||Yeah. Pay someone else to do it! Seriously. That is not a glib smartass comment. Tinting the Fiero is a pain in the ayass. If you insist on DIY, you will need to remove both seatbelts, the interior trim panels, and try not to get carpet fibers on the tint as you put it in. And if you do it wrong, you got no one to blame but yourself. <BR>A reputable shop shouldn't charge more then $100 to do rear and side windows with a lifetime guarantee. Everytime I've atempted tinting, I've thrown out $30 in materials.<BR>Pay a professional. It's so worth it.<P>Stimpy stole my post also. I hate it when people say i can't do things, but tinting windows is aggravtng.Tinting shops have all the tools and if they mees up it doesn't come out of your pocket.<P>AIR POCKETS SUCK||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FieroGTRacingInc||01-25-2001||06:48 PM||||--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>Yeah. Pay someone else to do it! Seriously. That is not a glib smartass comment. Tinting the Fiero is a pain in the ayass. If you insist on DIY, you will need to remove both seatbelts, the interior trim panels, and try not to get carpet fibers on the tint as you put it in. And if you do it wrong, you got no one to blame but yourself. <BR>A reputable shop shouldn't charge more then $100 to do rear and side windows with a lifetime guarantee. Everytime I've atempted tinting, I've thrown out $40 in materials and spent $60 at chriopractors recovering from playing Twister in the tiny interior.<P>Pay a professional. It's so worth <P>THIS IS NOT A JOKE........<BR>THIS IS A WARNING.........<BR>BEWARE....................<BR>AND....<BR>BECAREFUL.................<BR><P>------------------<BR>1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
Z||000011||TheHans||01-25-2001||07:06 PM||||I did my truck windows over the summer with the darkest tint available. Not Mirrrored.<BR>If you take your time and follow the directions (exactly) it should turn out alright. Mine is perfect with no bubbles thus far. <BR>I used a hairdrier to keep it pliable and to get the glass a pretty decent temperature.<BR>Did you get the self-adhesive or the stuff that you have to add the adhesive, or the cheap static stuff.<BR>Get a squeegie -sp? don't use anything hard like a card. It will scratch the surface of the plastic. I took my time and reaped the benefit. Just my experience.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Lisa 86 2m4||01-25-2001||07:45 PM||||It's really inexpensive to have it done professionally. I went to a place here in Fl, the quote was $60 for regular tint (sides & rear) or $95 for teh refelctive mirror tint.  As soon as I polish out some of the scratches, I'm getting the reflective. Had no idea it was so cheap!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex2004||01-25-2001||08:25 PM||||I tinted the windows in my fiero, just for temporary though.  I was really bored, and wanted to do something to it, so i had mom take me to autozone, and i bought some cheap static cling $8 tint.  I actually did a decent job, the tint is 20% limo tint, but it isn't very dark.  There are very little bubles in it that are just from dust and litte fibers but nothing big, i bought a squegy type thing and used that, i had enough to do the sides and rear, but the rear window is hard, and it really crappy.  There are bubles everywhere, and it's hard to get them out of the defroster lines.  I am going to have it done professionaly.  I called 2 places near me and they both wanted $130 for the Fiero, so i called back the next day just to be sure and they said the same. Oh, i also used a hair dryer to heat my windows. <BR>Alex...<p>[This message has been edited by Alex2004 (edited 01-25-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000014||fiero56||01-25-2001||11:51 PM||||I tinted the windows on Formula. Cost me $20 for enough film for both windows. Takes 10-15 minutes per window, really isn't difficult. Just make sure the glass is VERY clean, you keep everything wet, and you don't allow the film to fold in on itself as you apply it to the glass. Happened to me, so the passenger side needs to be re-done next summer. I've only had teh tint on for several months so I don't have a long term update, but it still looks good with no problems yet.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierofreek2001||01-25-2001||11:53 PM||||it is very much a waste of time to do it yourself, because in about a years time, it will be purple, and bubbly, and then it will cost more to take it off , if you have it done professionally... just have someone do it for you, i had a shop near me do it, and i get a lifetime warranty on it.. its the only way to go.. cost 140$    looks excellent<BR>steve <A HREF="http://www.fierosteve.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierosteve.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||sqoach||01-26-2001||11:53 AM||||haha i got purple tint in the rear window. also on the top of the windshield. a few bubbles here and there. you can tell it was a non-professional job. any good way to get that sh!t off? i got myself a little scraper tool  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000017||rogergarrison||01-26-2001||12:35 PM||||its really ez, I just did the ones in my Transport minivan in a half hour.<P>take off door panel is easiest but not necessary<P>clean the window spotless<P>spray windex (or mix of water and a few drops of dish wash liquid) on the glass and on the sticky side of film while its laying flat.<P>lay it on window makeing sure its past the edges all around, then spray more on face of film.<P>I use a plastic body filler spreader, its stiff and works excellent. BUY A BRAND NEW ONE SO IT WONT SCRATCH<P>make one pass across the middle of glass and work top half up and bottom half down till bubbles are gone<P>trim with pointed xacto knife around edges, keep squeegeing and towelling gently till its all down n dry.\<P>DONT ROLL IT UP OR DOWN FOR A DAY OR TWO.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||TheHans||01-26-2001||05:57 PM||||I've heard that when you may get a bubble or two in the tint-<BR>cut a small hole in the tint with a razor.<BR>place a drop of super glue in the hole and press it down.   <BR>Never tried it but it sounds good.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Phaeton||01-26-2001||11:50 PM||||My parts counter is very close to the tinting bay.  Observing the training of workers new to tinting certainly convinced me of the importance of experience.  EVERY trainee uses three to four times as much tint, taking it off and redoing, and this is in a shop with all the right equipment.<P>It all depends on what level of quality is acceptable.  A professional look is not achieved by accident on the first try.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000020||BigBoyToys||01-27-2001||12:40 AM||||If you do, do it follow Rogers advice!<BR>-Some scrape the entire surface with a razor to ensure no foreign particles are stuck.<BR>-More water is better than less since you are pressing much of it out as you squeege working from the center out. Plus it will allow the film to float on the glass while you work out any bubbles and get it into place. <BR>-Some trim the film at the edges back about 1/8 - 1/4 inch with a razor and you can apply a thin coat of clear nail polish to the cut edge to keep it from peeling back or as maintence.<BR>-Buy more than you expect to use and plan to do over or practice on a piece of plate glass or home window/fish tank...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> BBTs||||reg||6
